  "query_preview": "..\n\nWe currently reside under a broad upper level trough downstream of \nthe primary trough axis located over MN. We are in a relative lull \nin the pattern with thunderstorms along the main trough axis well to \nthe west and also to our east with the shortwave that passed through \novernight. Locally, a narrow axis of mid level forcing seen on water \nvapor imagery passing over the state this afternoon is expected to \nhelp touch off isolated showers and storms utilizing the 1000-2000 \nJ/kg of CAPE building up.  Shear remains weak, topping out around 20 \nknots currently so not expecting much in the way of stronger storms \norganizing. Wealth of dry air in the mid levels should also help \nkeep storms from getting too strong. SPC Meso Analysis does have \nDCAPE up around 1100 J/kg so any stronger storm will have \ncapabilities to produce a microburst. Additional isolated storms \nwill be possible overnight as models indicate some strengthening in \nthe mid level vorticity band over far southern MI. Loss of daytime \nheating should help minimize coverage during this time.\n\nSaturday again poses a risk of isolated showers and storms although \nlatest guidance has trended down with expected coverage. As the main \ntrough axis arrives, a weak cold front will be tracking across \nsouther MI. The system looks to be disjointed with strong mid level \nsupport arriving prior to 12Z, weak low level jet arriving after 12Z \ncoincident with a potential prefrontal trough which will push the \nbulk of the instability east of the region, before the front sweeps \nthrough in the early evening. There are enough pieces that could \nbriefly overlap with model estimates of around 1000-1500 J/kg of \nCAPE building early in the day. SPC Day 2 outlook is for general \nthunderstorms. \n\nSunday looks mostly dry as ridging to the south folds over through \nsouthern MI. We will be watching to the west as the next strong \nshortwave starts advancing across the Plains with surface low \npressure setting up around Iowa and the warm front extending across \nIL and IN. Models are already indicating ridge running type MCS \nactivity with a complex possibly pushing across southern MI Sunday \nnight into Monday morning. For this setup along the warm front, SPC \nhas already put locations south of a line from Grand Rapids to \nDetroit in a Slight Risk for severe weather. \n\nGeneral troughing and northwesterly flow across the region should \nlead to cooler temperatures for the latter half of the week, with \nhighs back down in the upper 70s. This setup will also lead to \nperiodic rain chances.",
